--- STEP 3  target-spec analysis ----------------------------------------------
3 | CREATED  | spec_analysis.log | 3a4b669faa2f | per-key direction / circuit expression / plausibility / what-to-save for all 4 keys, plus 4 tensions, a ranked knob list and 3 flow notes.
3 | FINDING  | tank C budget RECONCILED device-by-device, not guessed | - | back-solving f0=2.72465 GHz with L_diff=7.972 nH gives C_node=856 fF; the netlist accounts for it as MiM ~524 + coil Cpb 146 + varactor ~166 + core/wiring ~20 = ~856 fF. Every later sizing decision rests on this budget.
3 | FINDING  | the two "hardest" keys are ALIGNED, not opposed | - | shrinking XC0/XC1 raises OscFreq AND raises TuningRange (it raises the varactor's FRACTION of C_tank) AND raises OutputPower (R_p scales as w0^2). Only the varactor width trades f0 against TR. => the spec set is NOT over-constrained.
3 | FINDING  | OutputPower's usual lever is unavailable; a substitute exists | - | I_tail is blocked by Power (4.1% margin). But R_p,coil = (w0*L)^2/Rs rises x1.650 going 2.7247 -> 3.5 GHz at ZERO extra current = +2.17 dB vs the 1.33 dB needed. CONDITIONAL on non-coil tank losses not scaling as fast -> must be simulated every iteration, never assumed.
3 | FINDING  | tank Q is loss-dominated by something other than the coil | - | (4/pi)*I*R_p,coil predicts A1=2.05 V; measured A1=0.858 V => loaded R_tank_eff ~421 ohm vs 1010 ohm coil-only. ~half the Q is eaten by the varactors' l=0.5 low-Q channel and the MiM series rm3/rvia3.
3 | FINDING  | Power has exactly ONE device knob | - | Iref (300u) and VDD (1.8) are TESTBENCH quantities, not device parameters. The only device lever on Power is the XN4/XN5 width ratio, and it is effectively one-directional (may go down, not up).
3 | FINDING  | no over-constrained key flagged | - | all four reachable on this topology by the argument above. Start-up (gm*R_tank>1) is a precondition, not a key: a non-oscillating iteration is INVALID, not low-scoring.

